    "description": "Regional and community studies examine how social life differs across Iran's settlements, environments, languages, and economic settings. They connect national processes with the histories and everyday relationships of particular places without treating a province or community as socially uniform.",
    "tags": ["Regional diversity", "Rural communities", "Urban society", "Pastoralism", "Multilingualism", "Industrial history"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T04:42:58Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T04:42:58Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Regional and Community Studies in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iran through rural settlements, mobile pastoral communities, multilingual borderlands, industrial regions, and urban society."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "place-and-social-life",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why the local scale matters",
        "content": "A national description of Iranian society can conceal the different settings in which people live and work. Regional study asks how environment, settlement history, livelihood, and social affiliation interact in a particular place. Community study moves closer still, examining relationships within and between settlements. Neither scale should assume that a named region contains a single way of life. The historical geography of Iranian agriculture, for example, distinguishes humid Caspian farming environments, rain-fed cultivation, irrigated oases, and mountain grazing. These settings create different practical problems of water access, land use, and seasonal work. [s1]\n\nGeography supplies a starting point, not a complete social explanation. Eckart Ehlers's agricultural survey explicitly considers socioeconomic organization alongside ecological constraints. Its value today is as a historical framework: descriptions of older cultivation practices should not be read as an inventory of contemporary farming technology. Regional comparison becomes more informative when it asks how institutions and markets interact with environmental conditions rather than attributing every difference to climate. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "rural-and-mobile-communities",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Villages and mobile livelihoods",
        "content": "The Persian word deh means village, but a village is more than a collection of buildings. Encyclopaedia Iranica's account describes it as a unit of social and territorial affiliation, sometimes composed of related lineages and later arrivals. It also identifies a methodological difficulty: official sources have not always drawn village boundaries consistently. A settlement that one record treats separately may appear elsewhere as part of a larger unit. Before comparing population or service provision, a researcher must establish what the named village actually includes. [s2]\n\nMobile pastoral communities require a different geographical lens. Iranica's discussion of nomadism emphasizes seasonal movement between mountain grazing areas and lower winter pastures. A community's relevant territory may therefore be a route and a set of seasonal destinations rather than one permanent settlement. Its account also describes changing livelihoods and settlement patterns, making clear why mobile pastoralism should be studied as a changing economic and social arrangement. Tribal affiliation, migration practice, and permanent residence should be investigated separately rather than treated as interchangeable labels. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "languages-and-borderlands",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Language networks across boundaries",
        "content": "Language offers another way to understand regional connections. The Max Planck Institute's Araxes-Iran atlas project examines a contact area extending across northern Iran and neighboring parts of the Caucasus, Turkey, and Iraq. Its description brings together Persian, Kurdish varieties, Tati, Talyshi, Azerbaijani, Armenian, and other languages within a study of shared linguistic features. The important point is the network of contact, not an assumption that every language occupies a neatly enclosed territory. [s4]\n\nFor community studies, this encourages questions about which languages people use at home, in trade, and in other settings. A linguistic map does not automatically answer questions about ethnic identification or political allegiance. Nor does a project's inventory of language varieties provide a census of speakers. Border-province studies and multilingual-community studies consequently overlap, but they have different subjects: one begins with a territorial setting, while the other follows language practices and connections. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "industrial-and-urban-settings",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Industry as a maker of communities",
        "content": "Industrial regions show especially clearly that local society is produced by investment, labor recruitment, and planning as well as by inherited settlement patterns. Reza Mortaheb's dissertation on Abadan examines the oil city's development between 1908 and 1933. Its inspected abstract describes housing divisions between British employees, international migrant workers, and local workers, and interprets planning as a means of industrial management and social control. This is a historically bounded argument about a company town, not a description of present-day Abadan. [s5]\n\nThe branches of this guide therefore offer complementary perspectives: Tehran for metropolitan social life; rural communities for settlement and land-based relationships; nomadic communities for mobility; border provinces for cross-boundary settings; multilingual communities for communication and identity; and industrial regions for the organization of work and residence. Reading them together helps frame a more precise question than whether a locality is simply traditional or modern: which relationships have changed, for whom, and under what conditions? [s1][s2][s3][s4][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4", "s5"]
      }
    ],
