    "description": "Tehran's social geography reflects the interaction of metropolitan growth, housing, migration, and unequal access to urban resources. Studies of its neighborhoods reveal both persistent spatial divisions and local relationships that a simple north–south contrast cannot fully describe.",
    "tags": ["Tehran", "Urban society", "Neighborhoods", "Spatial inequality", "Housing", "Migration"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T04:42:58Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T04:42:58Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Urban Society in Tehran: Neighborhoods and Inequality | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Read Tehran through its settlement history, neighborhood relationships, housing divisions, and unequal access to green space."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "settlement-and-metropolis",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A metropolis with a longer local history",
        "content": "Tehran stands on the southern foothills of the Alborz mountains, along the northern edge of Iran's arid central plateau. Its metropolitan importance can obscure an earlier relationship with Ray, the older urban center to its south. Xavier de Planhol's historical geography describes Tehran as a settlement of gardens and orchards that supplied neighboring Ray before exceptional historical circumstances elevated its importance. This history helps explain why the capital should be understood as a changing inhabited landscape, rather than a city created entirely by modern expansion. [s1]\n\nGrowth also reorganized social space. A 1987 study by Hooshang Amirahmadi and Ali Kiafar connected Tehran's expansion and concentration of institutions with increasing separation between social classes. That is an interpretation of a particular historical period, not evidence that every contemporary neighborhood follows an identical pattern. It nevertheless identifies a durable research question: how do the advantages of metropolitan concentration become distributed among residents? [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "unequal-urban-resources",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading the north–south divide carefully",
        "content": "The association between affluent northern districts and disadvantaged southern areas remains useful when it is treated as a broad spatial pattern. A 2025 study of green infrastructure reports that Tehran's social divisions are also reflected in the distribution of vegetation in public and private properties. Its published summary describes an analysis combining occupational information with high-resolution satellite imagery. Green space therefore appears in this research as part of urban inequality, alongside the more familiar subjects of employment and housing. [s3]\n\nSuch findings require careful interpretation. Vegetation visible in an image does not automatically establish that a resident can enter, use, or comfortably reach a particular space. Public and private greenery have different social meanings. Similarly, a broad north–south pattern cannot describe every household or explain an individual's circumstances. The study supports attention to unequal environmental resources; its inspected summary does not justify precise claims here about park access, health effects, or neighborhood-level quantities. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "neighborhood-experience",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Separation and belonging can coexist",
        "content": "Research on Razavieh, a neighborhood in the Tehran metropolis, adds residents' experiences to the spatial picture. A 2019 study by Hashem Dadashpoor and Mohammad Ghazaie reports that low-income households and migrants were pushed toward the area, while residents experienced both disadvantages and forms of internal social connection. Its accessible discussion distinguishes the difficulties of segregation from relationships that make life within a segregated setting more tolerable. Strong local ties therefore should not be taken as evidence that wider exclusion is absent. [s4]\n\nThis neighborhood perspective also guards against treating Tehran as one homogeneous urban society. A metropolitan label can conceal different housing situations, social networks, and relationships with officials. It is equally important to distinguish the administrative city from the wider metropolitan region when interpreting research or population figures. The Razavieh study is evidence about a particular setting, not a representative survey of all Tehran residents. Read alongside the historical and environmental studies, it suggests that urban belonging involves both nearby relationships and access to opportunities beyond the neighborhood. [s2][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
